<CarpeItDoc
  title="CarpeIt — Privacy Policy"
  description="How CarpeIt, the water sports gear rental app, collects, uses and protects your data."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    CarpeIt connects people who rent water sports gear with the riders, schools and shops who
    lend it. To do that it has to know who you are, what you list and book, and what you say to
    the people you rent with. This page explains exactly what that is, who else handles it, and
    how to get it deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    CarpeIt is operated by {carpeitOperator.name} (NIPC {carpeitOperator.nipc}),
    {carpeitOperator.address}, which is the controller of your personal data for the purposes
    of the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). You can reach us at
    <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=CarpeIt%20privacy">hey@davidferreira.dev</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What CarpeIt collects</h2>
  <h3>Your account</h3>
  <p>
    Your email address and a display name, from the way you sign in: Sign in with Apple, Google,
    or a one-time code sent to your email. If you use Sign in with Apple and hide your email,
    CarpeIt only sees Apple's relay address. When you accept the Terms and confirm you are 18
    or over, CarpeIt records which version you accepted and when.
  </p>
  <h3>Your listings</h3>
  <p>
    What you describe about your gear, its photos, price, deposit and availability, and where it
    can be picked up. Photos are resized and stripped of their metadata on your phone before
    upload, so the location, time and device details a camera records are never sent. Your
    listing shows an approximate area, rounded to about a kilometre. The exact pickup address is
    shared only with a renter whose booking you have accepted.
  </p>
  <h3>Your bookings and payments</h3>
  <p>
    The gear, dates, prices, fees, deposits and status of every booking you make or receive.
    Card payments are handled by Stripe: CarpeIt never sees or stores your card number. CarpeIt
    keeps a record of each payment's amount and status. If you lend gear, Stripe collects the
    identity and bank details it needs to pay you out, under its own terms.
  </p>
  <h3>Your conversations and handovers</h3>
  <p>
    Messages and photos you send to other users, the photo checks you take at pickup and return,
    reviews you write, and reports you file. Handover photos are kept with the rental record and
    are visible to both people on that rental, because they are the evidence if something goes
    wrong.
  </p>
  <h3>Business details</h3>
  <p>
    If you set up a school or shop profile: your business name, type and website, which are
    public, and any legal name or tax identifier you provide, which are not.
  </p>
  <h3>Your device</h3>
  <p>
    A push notification token, if you turn notifications on. If the app crashes, freezes or
    uses unusual amounts of battery or storage, it sends CarpeIt a diagnostic report: what
    went wrong inside the app, and your app and iOS versions. Reports sent while you're signed
    in are linked to your account. CarpeIt uses them only to find and fix problems.
  </p>

  <h2>What CarpeIt does not do</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>No advertising, and no advertising identifiers.</li>
    <li>No tracking across other apps or websites, and no analytics SDKs.</li>
    <li>No selling or renting your data to anyone.</li>
    <li>No access to your contacts, and no background location. The only location CarpeIt has is the pickup point an owner chooses to enter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is used</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>To provide the service</strong> (performance of a contract): your account, listings, bookings, payments, messages, handover records and notifications.</li>
    <li><strong>To keep the marketplace safe</strong> (legitimate interests): reviewing reports, blocking abuse, resolving damage disputes, and preventing fraud.</li>
    <li><strong>To meet legal obligations</strong>: keeping payment and booking records for accounting and tax purposes, and the record of your acceptance of the Terms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who else handles it</h2>
  <p>CarpeIt relies on a small number of service providers, each processing data only to run the service:</p>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Supabase</strong> hosts CarpeIt's database, file storage and sign-in.</li>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong> processes payments, deposits and owner payouts, under <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe's privacy policy</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple</strong> and <strong>Google</strong> provide sign-in if you choose them. Apple delivers push notifications on iPhone, and Google (Firebase Cloud Messaging) on Android.</li>
    <li>An <strong>email delivery provider</strong> sends your sign-in codes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some of these providers process data outside the European Economic Area, including in the
    United States. Where they do, the transfer is covered by the European Commission's Standard
    Contractual Clauses or the EU–US Data Privacy Framework.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other CarpeIt users see what the service needs them to: your display name, rating, listings
    and reviews publicly; your messages and handover photos only on rentals you share with them;
    your exact pickup address only once you've accepted their booking.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    Your account, listings and profile are kept while your account is open. Some records have
    their own limits, and are deleted automatically when they run out:
  </p>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Messages and handover photos of a rental</strong>: 3 years after the rental ends or is cancelled, the period in which a claim about it can usually still be made.</li>
    <li><strong>Messages about a listing that never became a booking</strong>: 12 months after the last message.</li>
    <li><strong>Reports</strong>: 3 years after they are filed.</li>
    <li><strong>Crash and diagnostic reports</strong>: 90 days, including after you delete your account.</li>
    <li><strong>Bookings, payments, fees and invoices</strong>: 10 years, as Portuguese accounting and tax law requires.</li>
    <li><strong>Your acceptance of the Terms</strong>: as long as the account exists, and afterwards as long as a claim about it could be made.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you delete your account, your login, email, avatar, listings, listing photos, pickup
    addresses, saved gear, notification tokens and business details are removed straight away.
    Records of rentals you took part in, and the payments, messages, reviews and handover photos
    attached to them, are kept for the periods above so the other person still has their record
    and so CarpeIt can meet its accounting obligations. Your name on them is replaced with
    “Deleted user”.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    Open CarpeIt, go to Profile, and tap <strong>Delete account</strong>. It happens straight
    away. If you have a rental in progress, finish or cancel it first. Requests that nobody has
    answered yet are withdrawn for you. You can also email us to ask for deletion.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for a copy of your data, ask for it to be corrected or deleted, object to or ask
    to restrict how it is used, and ask for it in a portable format. Email <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=CarpeIt%20privacy">hey@davidferreira.dev</a> and
    we'll respond within a month. If you're not satisfied, you can complain to the Portuguese data
    protection authority, the <a href="https://www.cnpd.pt">CNPD</a>, or the authority where you
    live.
  </p>

  <h2>Age</h2>
  <p>
    CarpeIt is for adults. You must be 18 or older to use an account, because renting and
    lending involves payments and binding agreements, and you confirm this in the app before
    you start. If we learn that an account belongs to someone under 18, we close it.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be published here with a new date at the top,
    and significant changes will be announced in the app.
  </p>
</CarpeItDoc>
